Subject: [PATCH] network: listed loopback ports answer as closed

--aliasmode-closed-loopback-ports=<ports and a-b ranges> makes the listed
ports on 127.0.0.0/8, [::1] and localhost answer a web page as a closed
port does, so a page cannot find the launcher's or another local
service's open ports by timing or by the error it gets.

The network service holds one port bound on 127.0.0.1 and on [::1] (IPv4
alone where [::1] cannot be bound) and never listens on it. A page's
request to a listed loopback port is sent to that port instead, before
the request is made, so the operating system refuses it as it refuses
any closed port: the same error and timing as an unused port, and no
connection reaches the service on the listed port. This covers fetch,
XHR, subresources, iframes and CORS preflights (URLLoader), WebSockets,
and TURN over TCP and TLS. The local network access checks and prompts
are unchanged and still see the URL the page asked for.

Only requests from an http(s) page, or an opaque origin one created,
whose address space is more public than loopback are changed. Requests
from the browser itself, from extensions and from pages on loopback are
untouched. TURN connections carry no initiator, so every page's TURN
connection to a listed port is closed, unless its top-frame site has
another scheme. The switch reaches the network service through the
common child switches.

Without the switch nothing changes.

